WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
Hi everyone. My project is
called Planting Intelligence.

2
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.900
The idea is to explore how AI
can help turn everyday design

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.160
requests into practical planting
strategies, using professional planting

4
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:15.680
knowledge.

5
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.880
And more importantly, how it can help
us revise those strategies when design

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.220
priorities change.

7
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:27.427
So, the first question is,
what is planting design?

8
00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:33.027
Basically, it's about choosing plants,
deciding where they go, and how they work

9
00:00:33.047 --> 00:00:38.147
together. To do this, we need to
understand the site conditions, like

10
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:43.527
sunlight, soil, and other
elements, as well as the

11
00:00:43.547 --> 00:00:46.207
characteristics of different plants.

12
00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:50.587
Then we arrange them to create
spaces and seasonal changes.

13
00:00:50.607 --> 00:00:53.027
The goals depend on the project.

14
00:00:53.407 --> 00:00:57.667
It could be about visual experience,
ecological value, or maintenance.

15
00:00:58.247 --> 00:01:01.327
And sometimes we need to balance
these different priorities.

16
00:01:03.237 --> 00:01:08.867
Planting design is also an important part
of professional landscape architecture

17
00:01:08.887 --> 00:01:14.067
practice. On the left is a real
design process from a landscape

18
00:01:14.087 --> 00:01:16.527
architecture firm in Seattle.

19
00:01:16.547 --> 00:01:21.027
It shows how professional design
follows a structured workflow.

20
00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:26.497
On the right is a summary of the
planting design process in four main

21
00:01:26.517 --> 00:01:27.267
steps.

22
00:01:28.607 --> 00:01:33.027
We start by understanding the
site and the design brief.

23
00:01:33.047 --> 00:01:38.747
Then we select the plants and
develop the planting composition.

24
00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:43.900
And finally, we review and revise
the design based on feedback.

25
00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.070
Now, let's imagine we are working on
a planting project at East Park in

26
00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:54.660
Vancouver. The site is in Olympic
Village, and it's a real site, but I've

27
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.880
made up three requests for this example.

28
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.720
So the first request would be the
residents, and they say, we want lots of

29
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:08.160
yellow flowers. Then an ecological
adviser asks, can we support

30
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.000
pollinators across different seasons too?

31
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:17.920
And the maintenance team also says, that
sounds great, but we can't keep watering

32
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.880
and replacing plants.

33
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.740
So everyone wants something different,
and all of these requests make sense in

34
00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.920
this space. But how do we bring
them together into one planting

35
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.000
design?

36
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:36.527
So, let's see what tools we have
today to help with these requests.

37
00:02:36.547 --> 00:02:42.167
We could use a general-purpose
language model, like ChatGPT.

38
00:02:42.187 --> 00:02:46.587
We can open the chat box
and describe our goals,

39
00:02:47.327 --> 00:02:50.767
and GPT will give us suggestions
about planting strategies.

40
00:02:50.787 --> 00:02:56.107
Or we could use a
planting-specific AI platform, like

41
00:02:56.127 --> 00:02:57.087
db.garden.

42
00:02:57.987 --> 00:03:03.527
We enter a design vision, and
db.garden will give us ranked plant

43
00:03:03.547 --> 00:03:04.547
recommendations.

44
00:03:05.747 --> 00:03:11.507
Or professional CAD and BIM tools,
like RhinoLands, where designers can

45
00:03:11.527 --> 00:03:15.747
arrange plants and visualize
the designs in 2D or 3D.

46
00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:22.167
These tools are already useful, and each
gives us a different way to approach the

47
00:03:22.187 --> 00:03:27.607
problem. But our challenge now
isn't just getting a design

48
00:03:27.627 --> 00:03:28.347
proposal.

49
00:03:29.047 --> 00:03:31.987
It is about balancing
the three priorities,

50
00:03:32.687 --> 00:03:38.167
understanding the trade-offs, and
revising the designs when those priorities

51
00:03:38.187 --> 00:03:38.967
change.

52
00:03:40.233 --> 00:03:44.373
So, this is my idea,
Planting Intelligence.

53
00:03:44.393 --> 00:03:49.853
Instead of just focusing
on one design solution,

54
00:03:50.573 --> 00:03:55.373
I want to explore how AI can help
us balance different priorities

55
00:03:55.393 --> 00:03:57.493
with different parties.

56
00:03:58.213 --> 00:04:03.033
We start with the same three requests,
but this time, the AI would use

57
00:04:03.053 --> 00:04:08.073
professional planting knowledge to
understand the conflicts and propose

58
00:04:08.093 --> 00:04:09.093
different strategies.

59
00:04:10.073 --> 00:04:15.953
For example, one might focus on seasonal
flowers, another on native planting,

60
00:04:16.073 --> 00:04:17.833
and another on low maintenance.

61
00:04:18.873 --> 00:04:23.433
We can compare what we gain
or give up with each option.

62
00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:29.253
And if we change a request or ask
for a better balance, the AI would

63
00:04:29.273 --> 00:04:34.393
reconsider the trade-offs and revise the
strategy using that planting knowledge.

64
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.760
But how would Planting
Intelligence actually work?

65
00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:46.540
In my idea, I'm not building
a new language model.

66
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.020
I'm building the planting
knowledge it can work with.

67
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.540
So I might start with two parts.

68
00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.710
A plant database tells us what
different plants need and what they

69
00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:05.010
can offer. A design logic base
explains how plants can work

70
00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:08.500
together to achieve different goals.

71
00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:13.640
When someone enters a request, the
system finds relevant knowledge from

72
00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.220
both and gives it to the language model.

73
00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:22.530
The idea is that the model can use this
knowledge to weigh different strategies

74
00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:25.630
and make better-informed decisions.

75
00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:30.390
So let's look at an example of how
these two types of knowledge could work

76
00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:31.540
together.

77
00:05:31.933 --> 00:05:35.233
We still use East Park as an example.

78
00:05:35.253 --> 00:05:38.553
We will start with the design logic.

79
00:05:38.573 --> 00:05:43.353
If we want to support
pollinators across seasons,

80
00:05:44.253 --> 00:05:46.833
we need plants that
flower at different times.

81
00:05:46.853 --> 00:05:52.273
Then the AI would use the plant
database to check their flowering

82
00:05:52.293 --> 00:05:55.253
seasons, water needs, and site conditions.

83
00:05:55.273 --> 00:06:00.533
This logic will help AI to make different

84
00:06:00.553 --> 00:06:06.093
decisions. For example, Oregon
grape is worth considering for

85
00:06:06.113 --> 00:06:11.233
spring flowers. Black-eyed Susan could
provide summer colour but needs some

86
00:06:11.253 --> 00:06:11.973
watering.

87
00:06:12.833 --> 00:06:17.953
And common sneezeweed needs
too much moisture, so we would

88
00:06:17.973 --> 00:06:19.593
exclude it.

89
00:06:20.573 --> 00:06:24.793
So we would bring together design
principles and plant information.

90
00:06:24.813 --> 00:06:30.593
This is the knowledge we want the language
model to use to weigh different options

91
00:06:30.613 --> 00:06:33.993
and explain why they
work or they don't work.

92
00:06:34.927 --> 00:06:39.167
We have the first prototype.

93
00:06:39.187 --> 00:06:44.297
For now, the prototype is a
rule-based demo, rather than an

94
00:06:44.317 --> 00:06:46.707
actual language model.

95
00:06:46.727 --> 00:06:50.487
We start with our three
requests from East Park.

96
00:06:50.507 --> 00:06:55.714
The tool shows us where everyone
agrees, where the goals might

97
00:06:55.734 --> 00:06:58.487
conflict, and what we still need to know.

98
00:06:58.507 --> 00:07:02.647
Then we can explore three
different planting strategies.

99
00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:05.607
But what if you don't like the results?

100
00:07:05.627 --> 00:07:10.727
Let's say you prefer Strategy
B, but you ask, can we reduce

101
00:07:10.747 --> 00:07:14.047
watering without losing
too much summer colour?

102
00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:19.261
The prototype checks the plants'
water needs and revises the strategy.

103
00:07:19.281 --> 00:07:24.753
Three plants are removed, while
Black-eyed Susan is kept as a conditional

104
00:07:24.773 --> 00:07:30.107
option. So now we can see what
changed and why, without losing

105
00:07:30.127 --> 00:07:33.207
sight of our original goals.

106
00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.860
We also want to see what happens if we
give the same feedback to the existing

107
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.360
tools. So the first one would be ChatGPT.

108
00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:47.660
It can suggest plant changes
and explain its choices,

109
00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.300
but the user cannot see the
logic behind GPT's choices.

110
00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.860
With db.garden, we can update
our design vision and get a new

111
00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.060
list of recommended plants.

112
00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:06.520
But with Planting Intelligence, we can
focus on the design strategy itself.

113
00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:10.840
If we reduce watering, which
plants should we remove?

114
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.080
What can we keep, and what
happens to our summer colour?

115
00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:21.720
The prototype shows these changes side by
side, with reasons based on plant data.

116
00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:26.900
So the difference I'm
exploring is not just

117
00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:32.440
recommending plants, but helping
designers understand and negotiate

118
00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.040
the trade-offs behind each decision.

119
00:08:36.833 --> 00:08:40.233
But this is a very simple demo model.

120
00:08:40.493 --> 00:08:41.573
So, what's next?

121
00:08:41.993 --> 00:08:47.433
I feel like the next step is to build
the actual Planting Intelligence system.

122
00:08:48.013 --> 00:08:53.753
I'll expand the planting knowledge base
and connect it to a language model.

123
00:08:54.253 --> 00:08:59.993
I also want to go beyond text,
allowing users to use images,

124
00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:03.193
videos, and site information as inputs.

125
00:09:03.573 --> 00:09:09.133
The model would use this information
and professional planting knowledge to

126
00:09:09.153 --> 00:09:12.833
develop, compare, and
revise different strategies.

127
00:09:13.933 --> 00:09:19.653
But the most important thing is to test
how much this knowledge can improve the

128
00:09:19.673 --> 00:09:25.193
model's decisions. So finally, I
want to make planting design more

129
00:09:25.213 --> 00:09:30.453
accessible, so anyone can explore
ideas and make informed decisions,

130
00:09:30.473 --> 00:09:33.533
even without professional
design experience.
